# SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
#
# PROBLEM: `boot` reads like an immediate action, but it is
# persistent configuration. Every neighboring verb acts now; this
# one quietly rewrites machine state that outlives the script.

description "boot: sounds immediate, acts persistent"
platform    dos
machine     stopped
timeout     2m

boot cdrom0 hdd0     # does NOT boot anything — it replaces the
                     # machine's persistent boot order (stopped
                     # machines only)...
start                # ...THIS boots, using the new order
wait  "Welcome"
enter "shutdown"
wait  stopped

# The reordering OUTLIVES this script: the machine keeps booting
# CD-first until another `boot` — or `apply` — restores it. That
# persistence matches insert/eject, but those verbs at least read
# as putting a thing somewhere; compare tenses:
#
#     start            act now
#     stop             act now
#     insert cdrom0 …  place a thing (persists, but reads that way)
#     boot cdrom0 hdd0 configure permanently  <- reads like "act now"
#
# A reader who knows QEMU's -boot flag will parse it correctly; a
# reader who knows English will expect the machine to start
# booting on that line.
#
# OPEN QUESTIONS: rename to make the configuration nature audible?
#     boot-order cdrom0 hdd0    # honest, but noun-first inside the
                                # imperative zone — bends the
                                # nouns-declare/verbs-act rule
#     reorder cdrom0 hdd0       # verb-first, loses the boot domain
#     set-boot cdrom0 hdd0      # verb-first and domain-bearing
# Or keep `boot` because scripts rarely need it (the blueprint's
# fall-through order covers the install pattern) and let its rarity
# bound the confusion? Weak counterpoint: rare verbs are exactly
# the ones read cold, without habit to correct the tense.
